{
  "requirement": "A successfully delivered search result MUST become outstanding, including\na result with no matches, and remain outstanding until accepted by\n`finish_search`. Failed searches and undelivered results MUST NOT create\noutstanding searches.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The two marked tests cover each obligation in `requirements.authoring.search_accounting.outstanding`.\n\n**Delivered results become outstanding, including empty ones.**\n- `src/authoring/searches.rs#mod[tests]/fn[delivered_searches_including_empty_results_need_explicit_completion]` runs a matching search, an empty search (\"absent\") and a repeated search. It asserts `outstanding_searches().len() == 3`, so the empty result counts.\n- The same test checks that `ripgrep` and `ast_grep` empty results each return `matches == []` with a valid `search_id`. A second identical call returns a different id.\n\n**Results stay outstanding until `finish_search` accepts them.**\n- The searches test finishes each result with `finish_search`. It asserts the outstanding list is empty only after all three are finished.\n- It also calls `finish_search` on a fresh `ResultPages` that never delivered the id. The call errors with \"delivered in this assessment\".\n- In `src/authoring/pages.rs#mod[tests]/fn[search_pages_are_accounted_only_when_delivered_and_replay_stays_closed]`, two rejected `finish_search` calls leave the first search outstanding. One uses an undelivered page's id. The other uses the first search's id with an invalid tag.\n- The next assertion is still `outstanding_searches().len() == 2`.\n- After the second page is finished and replayed, only the first id remains outstanding. It leaves the list only once `finish_search` accepts it.\n\n**Failed searches create no outstanding search.**\n- The searches test dispatches `ripgrep` with the invalid regex `(`. It asserts the call errors and `outstanding_searches()` is empty.\n- `dispatch_paged` applies `listing?` before `pages.deliver`, so failures of every tool share one path. A failed `ast_grep` call therefore needs no separate test.\n\n**Undelivered results create no outstanding search.**\n- In the pages test, the oversized `ripgrep` and `ast_grep` results leave an undelivered second page prepared with its own `search_id`. After delivering only the first page, `outstanding_searches()` equals exactly `[first id]`.\n- Delivering the second page via `next_page` raises the count to 2.\n- Replaying the token after `finish_search` does not re-open the search.\n\nThe assertions check actual outstanding sets, not just that the code ran. I found no gap.",
